---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Fg04ShadeMap from '../../../components/Fg04ShadeMap.astro';
import '../../../styles/fg04.css';

/*
 * Throwing Shade.
 *
 * Every figure on this page is a 2 m figure computed on 21 July 2026, 06:00 to
 * 20:00 EDT, from lidar flown April to May 2023. Source of record:
 * data/proof/fg04/statistics.json. Nothing here is rounded by hand.
 *
 * Two rules govern the copy, and tests/fg04-content.test.mjs enforces both:
 *   1. This maps shade. It never claims temperature, heat, or coolness.
 *   2. Every neighbourhood and street figure is published twice, measured and
 *      leaf-on corrected, side by side. The aircraft flew in spring, so a
 *      measured figure standing alone reports the month of the flight as a
 *      fact about the city.
 */
---

<Base
  title="Throwing Shade"
  description="Fifteen hourly frames of Toronto's ground shade on 21 July 2026, modelled at 2 m. Correcting a spring lidar flight for summer leaves changes the answer."
  ogImage="/social/og-throwing-shade.jpg"
  ogImageAlt="The same Toronto streets at 13:00 and 18:00 on 21 July 2026, with shaded ground shown in dark charcoal."
  ogImageWidth={1200}
  ogImageHeight={630}
  ogImageType="image/jpeg"
  guideTheme="fg04"
  articlePublished="2026-08-06"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Toronto Micro-Atlas', path: '/' }, { name: 'Field guides', path: '/guides/' }]}
  articleUpdated="2026-08-07"
>
  <div class="fg04">
    <article class="fg04-container">

      <header class="fg04-chapter">
        <span class="fg04-kicker">Shade, towers &amp; trees</span>
        <h1>Throwing Shade</h1>
        <!-- The lede names the reason a person is on this page before it names
             the instrument. "It is a hot afternoon" is a motivation, not a
             measurement, and it is allowlisted in tests/fg04-content.test.mjs
             for exactly that reason. The rule this guide keeps is that it makes
             no thermal CLAIM about the ground. Refusing to say why anyone wants
             shade was a wider rule than the claim needed, and it cost the guide
             its opening. -->
        <p class="fg04-lede">
          It is a hot afternoon and you want to be out of the sun. At one
          o'clock, four fifths of Toronto's ground stands in open sun. This
          maps where the other fifth is, hour by hour, across one modelled day.
        </p>
      </header>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Chapter one. Opens on the behaviour, not on the data. No numbers
           until the reader has been told what is being measured and, just as
           importantly, what is not.
           ============================================================ -->
      <section class="fg04-chapter" id="the-map-you-were-already-using">
        <span class="fg04-kicker">Chapter one</span>
        <h2>The map you were already using</h2>

        <p>
          You already do this. Walking a long block in July, you cross to the
          far side without deciding to, then cross back when the buildings run
          out. Nobody taught you the rule. You read the pavement ahead, saw
          where the light stopped, and moved.
        </p>

        <p>
          That habit is a map. You drew it from memory, it covers only the
          streets you walk, and it changes every time a tower goes up or a tree
          comes down. This guide draws the same map for the whole city, on one
          day of one year.
        </p>

        <div class="fg04-scope-note">
          <p>
            <strong>This guide maps shade, not temperature.</strong> Shade is a
            question of what stood between the ground and the sun at a given
            minute. That is all it measures. A shaded asphalt lot can be hotter
            than a sunny lawn, and nothing here measures heat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          Here is the whole city, averaged across every hour of the modelled
          day. The left figure is the surface the aircraft measured. The right
          figure is the one this guide publishes. Chapter three says why.
        </p>

        <div class="fg04-pair">
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--raw">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Measured, leaf-off</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">6.201</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              mean shaded hours of fifteen, citywide
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--corrected">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Leaf-on corrected</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">7.197</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              the same ground, with the trees given their summer leaves back
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============================================================
           The map. Placed here, after chapter one and before any number,
           because chapter one is where the reader is told what is being
           measured and what is not. A shade map shown before that invites
           the reading this guide spends nine limits refusing.

           Both surfaces, side by side. The correction reverses the ranking,
           so a reader who can see one has to be able to see the other.
           ============================================================ -->
      <Fg04ShadeMap />

      <!-- ============================================================
           Chapter two. The confirmed finding, and the one place the dawn and
           dusk frames get explained. Every later shaded-hour count in the
           guide depends on the reader having been told, once and plainly,
           that the fifteen frames are not fifteen equal hours.
           ============================================================ -->
      <section class="fg04-chapter" id="noon-is-the-cruellest-hour">
        <span class="fg04-kicker">Chapter two</span>
        <h2>Noon is the cruellest hour</h2>

        <p>
          It is not, quite. The sun over Toronto peaks at <strong>13:25
          EDT</strong> on 21 July, not at noon. Daylight saving takes an hour
          of the blame. The rest is Toronto sitting west of the meridian its
          time zone is named for. Shade is thinnest at the modelled hour
          nearest that peak, and that hour is <strong>13:00</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="fg04-pair">
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--raw">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Measured, leaf-off</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">10.73%</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              of Toronto's ground in shade at 13:00
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--corrected">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Leaf-on corrected</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">20.73%</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              of Toronto's ground in shade at 13:00, the day's minimum
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          Four fifths of the city stands in open sun at one in the afternoon.
          That is the generous reading. The surface the aircraft measured puts
          it nearer nine tenths.
        </p>

        <h3 class="fg04-kicker">What fifteen frames actually means</h3>

        <p>
          The day runs from <strong>06:00</strong> to <strong>20:00 EDT</strong>
          on <strong>21 July 2026</strong>, sampled once an hour. Fifteen
          frames, computed on a <strong>2 m grid</strong> across the whole
          municipality. Every figure in this guide counts how many of those
          fifteen frames found a patch of ground in shadow.
        </p>

        <p>
          Fifteen frames are not fifteen equal hours. At 06:00 the sun sits
          0.38&deg; above the horizon, at or below the point the model treats
          as sunrise, so that frame finds the city <strong>shaded
          everywhere</strong>. Every patch of ground in Toronto scores one
          shaded hour before anything about it is measured. The 20:00 frame is
          nearly as generous: 83.8% of ground in shade on the measured surface
          and 85.2% corrected, with the sun under
          8&deg;. So seven shaded hours means seven frames <em>of the modelled
          day</em>, and two of them came free. It does not mean seven hours of
          useful shade, and this guide never says it does.
        </p>

        <p>
          The hours that settle anything are the ones in the middle. That is
          why 13:00 is the frame worth arguing about.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Chapter three. The reversal, and the reason the whole correction
           apparatus exists. The claim is narrow on purpose: the order of the
           league table changes, the distances between its top entries do not
           open up, and downtown keeps fourth and fifth. Anything stronger
           than that is not in the data.
           ============================================================ -->
      <section class="fg04-chapter" id="the-month-the-plane-flew">
        <span class="fg04-kicker">Chapter three</span>
        <h2>The month the plane flew</h2>

        <p>
          Ask which part of Toronto sits in the most shade and the measured
          surface answers immediately: downtown. Towers throw long shadows and
          there are a great many towers in a small space. The three shadiest
          neighbourhoods are all tower districts, and the ranking looks
          settled.
        </p>

        <p>
          Correct the same surface for summer leaves and the top of the table
          turns over. All three of the new leaders are leafy midtown
          neighbourhoods, and downtown leaves the top three entirely.
        </p>

        <div class="fg04-ranking">
          <div class="fg04-ranking__col fg04-ranking__col--raw">
            <p class="fg04-ranking__head">Measured, leaf-off</p>
            <ol>
              <li data-moved><span>Yonge-Bay Corridor</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">10.90</span></li>
              <li data-moved><span>Downtown Yonge East</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">10.60</span></li>
              <li data-moved><span>Church-Wellesley</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">10.58</span></li>
              <li><span>Wellington Place</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">10.24</span></li>
              <li><span>North St. James Town</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">9.81</span></li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <div class="fg04-ranking__col fg04-ranking__col--corrected">
            <p class="fg04-ranking__head">Leaf-on corrected</p>
            <ol>
              <li data-moved><span>Lawrence Park South</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">11.30</span></li>
              <li data-moved><span>Rosedale-Moore Park</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">11.25</span></li>
              <li data-moved><span>Mount Pleasant East</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">11.17</span></li>
              <li><span>Church-Wellesley</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">11.07</span></li>
              <li><span>Yonge-Bay Corridor</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">11.01</span></li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          The reason is the aircraft. Every lidar flight ever flown over
          Toronto has been flown in spring, when the ground is visible and the
          survey is cheap to fly. This one flew <strong>April to May
          2023</strong>. A building looks the same in April as it does in July.
          A tree does not. So the measured surface records every tower at its
          full height and every deciduous crown as a bare frame, and the
          ranking it produces is partly a picture of the month the plane flew.
        </p>

        <p>
          That is the whole argument for correcting it, and it is why this
          guide publishes two numbers everywhere instead of one.
        </p>

        <h3 class="fg04-kicker">How big is the reversal, honestly</h3>

        <p>
          Small, and worth saying so. Church-Wellesley and Yonge-Bay Corridor
          are still fourth and fifth once corrected, and the whole corrected
          top five spans <strong>0.29 hours</strong>, about seventeen minutes of
          the modelled day. The order changed. The distances did not open up.
          Downtown did not stop being shady, and on the other end of the table
          it beats Downsview, at 3.57 measured and 3.90 corrected, by a wide
          margin.
        </p>

        <p>
          The correction is also the generous end of the range. Ground under a
          leaf-on crown is counted as shaded for the whole modelled day, and
          real crowns are not solid: light comes through them, and low sun
          arrives sideways underneath them. The true answer sits somewhere
          between these two columns, nearer the right one in July. That is the
          honest claim, and it is smaller than a headline would like.
        </p>

        <h3 class="fg04-kicker">Towers still work</h3>

        <p>
          The shadiest single arterial in Toronto is <strong>York
          Street</strong>, and it barely moves between the two surfaces.
        </p>

        <div class="fg04-pair">
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--raw">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Measured, leaf-off</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">11.70</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              York Street, mean shaded hours of fifteen
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--corrected">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Leaf-on corrected</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">11.74</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              York Street again, moved by four hundredths of an hour
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          York Street sits in a trench of towers and almost none of its shade
          comes from trees, so the correction has nothing to add. The point of
          this chapter is not that towers fail to shade a street. They shade it
          reliably, and the aircraft measured them correctly the first time.
          The point is that towers and trees buy shade differently, and a
          spring flight can only see one of them properly.
        </p>

        <h3 class="fg04-kicker">The shadiest arterial outside downtown</h3>

        <p>
          The ranking outside downtown requires at least <strong>1 km of
          sampled centreline</strong>. Without that floor, a 309.7 m Oxton
          Avenue feature tagged as an arterial wins a citywide superlative it
          is too short to carry. With the floor applied, <strong>St. Clair
          Avenue East</strong> is the leader: 9.99 mean shaded hours on the
          measured surface and 10.65 after the leaf-on correction, over 1.05
          km of sampled centreline.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Chapter four. The finding is a contrast between two bands, never a
           shortage. The pre-registered shortage condition failed, and this is
           where the guide says so and retires the title it was built around.
           ============================================================ -->
      <section class="fg04-chapter" id="the-bare-road-and-the-shaded-walk">
        <span class="fg04-kicker">Chapter four</span>
        <h2>The bare road and the shaded walk</h2>

        <p>
          This guide was built to answer one question: does Toronto have a
          shade shortage on the streets people walk along? The test was written
          down before any citywide surface existed. A street segment counts as
          shade-poor if its samples are shaded in fewer than five of the
          fifteen frames, on median, which given the free frames at either end
          of the day works out at one or two shaded hours between 07:00 and
          19:00. A citywide shortage means at least <strong>25%</strong> of
          Toronto's arterial kilometres are shade-poor. Both numbers were
          fixed in advance.
        </p>

        <p>
          Of the 1,128.6 arterial kilometres inside the city, here is the
          answer, measured where people actually stand.
        </p>

        <div class="fg04-pair">
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--raw">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Walk band, measured</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">8.49%</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              95.9 km of arterial shade-poor on the sidewalk
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--corrected">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Walk band, corrected</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">5.26%</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              59.4 km, against a threshold of 25%
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          Move the same measurement off the sidewalk and onto the traffic lanes
          and the picture changes completely.
        </p>

        <div class="fg04-pair">
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--raw">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Road band, measured</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">42.04%</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              474.4 km of arterial shade-poor over the roadway
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--corrected">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Road band, corrected</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">39.50%</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              445.8 km, seven and a half times the share on the sidewalk beside it
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          That is the finding, and it is a contrast rather than a shortage.
          Toronto's arterial roads are shaded where people walk and bare where
          the cars drive. Street trees lean over the sidewalk they were planted
          beside and stop well short of the centre line, so the asphalt keeps
          the sun all day and the pavement beside it does not.
        </p>

        <h3 class="fg04-kicker">The hypothesis this guide came to test is dead</h3>

        <p>
          <strong>5.26% against a threshold of 25%.</strong> The guide went
          looking for a citywide shade shortage on the walking network, wrote
          the threshold down before computing anything, and did not find one.
          It missed by nearly a factor of five. The title this guide would have carried
          if the condition had held, The Great Toronto Shade Shortage, is
          retired rather than kept back for a future run.
        </p>

        <p>
          A guide that declares its own hypothesis dead is the most
          trustworthy thing in this atlas. The alternative was to quietly swap
          in the road band, publish 39.50% as though it were the sidewalk
          finding, and keep the better title.
        </p>

        <h3 class="fg04-kicker">Which band counts, and who chose it</h3>

        <p>
          A sidewalk on a Toronto arterial sits roughly <strong>8 to 15 m</strong>
          from the centre line, so that is the band the shortage test uses.
          The original plan sampled 6 m out, which turned out to be the roadway
          itself: only 0.23% of that strip falls under canopy, against 6.64% of
          Toronto's ground generally. Measuring 6 m from a centre line measures
          the road, and a claim about walking has to be measured where walking
          happens.
        </p>

        <p>
          The band was chosen after both sets of numbers were visible, and it
          decides the title, so it is disclosed here rather than presented as
          though only one band had ever been computed. Both bands are published
          above for that reason. The road band clears 25% on either surface,
          and calling that a shortage for people on foot would be false.
        </p>

        <h3 class="fg04-kicker">The places that really are sunless</h3>

        <p>
          A failed citywide condition does not mean nowhere is short of shade.
          The sunniest arterial in Toronto is <strong>Staines Road</strong> in
          northeast Scarborough. It reads 1.85 mean shaded hours of fifteen on
          the measured surface and 1.86 after the leaf-on correction, because
          the correction found almost no canopy there to give leaves back to.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>533 of Toronto's 8,432 transit stops</strong> record no
          modelled shade during the usable day, on either surface. That is one
          stop in sixteen. Their single shaded hour is the 06:00 frame, the one
          that counts the whole city as shaded before anything is measured, so
          a rider waiting at any hour worth waiting in stands in open sun.
        </p>

        <p>Three of the 533, to put an address on it:</p>

        <ul class="fg04-places">
          <li>Midland Ave at Emblem Crt</li>
          <li>Warden Ave at Roper Rd (North Commuter Parking Lot)</li>
          <li>Albion Rd at Carrier Dr</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          This is the finding that held. The citywide shortage did not, and the
          improvement-area gap came to twelve minutes. Nearly sixty kilometres
          of shade-poor sidewalk and 533 bare transit stops is smaller than the
          story this guide set out to tell, and it is bigger than a list of
          addresses.
        </p>

        <p>
          Across all 8,432 transit stops, the mean is 6.00 shaded frames on
          the measured surface and 7.00 after the leaf-on correction.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Chapter five. A published null, and deliberately the shortest
           section on the page.

           The numbers stay inline rather than going into a .fg04-pair block.
           That is a judgement about weight, not a shortcut: the paired figure
           is the loudest component in this guide, and giving a gap of twelve
           minutes the same frame as the 13:00 finding would let the layout
           make a claim the copy is refusing to make.
           ============================================================ -->
      <section class="fg04-chapter" id="what-the-map-did-not-find">
        <span class="fg04-kicker">Chapter five</span>
        <h2>What the map did not find</h2>

        <p>
          This guide expected shade to be handed out unevenly across the city
          and set out to show it. Toronto designates 33 Neighbourhood
          Improvement Areas. On the measured surface, ground inside them
          averages 6.027 shaded hours of fifteen against 6.246 outside, a gap
          of 0.219. After the leaf-on correction, the pair is 7.034 inside and
          7.240 outside, a gap of <strong>0.206 hours</strong>. That is about
          twelve minutes, or 1.4% of the modelled day. The direction is the one you would
          expect. The size does not carry a chapter, and dressing twelve
          minutes as a finding about inequality would be dishonest. Two things
          bound what the null is worth: the improvement-area designation is a
          dated instrument that does not track today's canopy, and ground shade
          at 2 m on one July day is not the only way to ask who has trees. This
          is reported because it was tested, not because it found anything.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Chapter six. One measured winter frame, kept deliberately narrow.

           Written as shadow geometry only. The winter reversal is interesting
           precisely because of what a January shadow does to a person
           standing in it, and that is the one thing this guide is not
           entitled to say, so it says so instead of reaching for it.

           One frame supports a statement about midday coverage. It does not
           support a statement about how long winter shade lasts.
           ============================================================ -->
      <section class="fg04-chapter" id="the-same-city-in-january">
        <span class="fg04-kicker">Chapter six</span>
        <h2>The same city in January</h2>

        <p>
          Everything above describes one day in July. At 12:00 EST on 21
          January, the sun sits 26.24&deg; above the horizon. At the nearest
          modelled hour to solar noon in July, it sits 66.19&deg;. The lower
          January angle throws longer shadows, so more of the same ground is
          shaded at that frame. One frame says how much ground is shaded at
          midday. It does not say how long the shade stays there.
        </p>

        <p>
          Whether you want that shadow is not a question this map can answer.
          It records where the sun reaches the ground. It says nothing about
          what standing there is like, in either season.
        </p>

        <div class="fg04-pair">
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--raw">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Measured, leaf-off</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">47.22%</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              ground shaded at 12:00 EST in January; this is the number to read
              because January trees are bare
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--corrected">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Leaf-on corrected</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">53.27%</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              a summer-foliage counterfactual, printed beside the measurement
              rather than presented as January's canopy
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          In July, the comparable 13:00 frame puts 10.73% of ground in shade
          on the measured surface and 20.73% after the leaf-on correction.
          January's measured 47.22% is just under half, not most of the city.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Limits.

           A reader has to be able to disagree with this guide for a stated
           reason, so each item names what it would change if it mattered to
           you, rather than hedging in general. The two sentences about
           temperature are reproduced here verbatim because this is the
           section a sceptical reader opens first, and the copy contract
           matches them exactly.
           ============================================================ -->
      <section class="fg04-limits" id="limits">
        <h2>What this map cannot tell you</h2>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>This guide maps shade, not temperature.</strong>
            A shaded asphalt lot can be hotter than a sunny lawn, and nothing
            here measures heat. Every figure above says where the sun was
            blocked and nothing at all about what standing there is like.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>The correction is the generous end of the range.</strong>
            Ground under a leaf-on crown is counted as shaded for the whole
            modelled day. Real crowns are not solid: light comes through them,
            and low sun arrives sideways underneath them. Wherever this guide
            prints two figures, the truthful answer sits between them, nearer
            the corrected one in July.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>The correction is measured, not assumed.</strong> It raises
            bare canopy by a step at 3 m, and <strong>99.8%</strong> of the
            pixels it raised took a height measured from a neighbouring crown
            rather than a default. It still fixes canopy the flight missed
            entirely better than canopy the flight caught halfway.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>The land cover is 2018 and the lidar is 2023.</strong> They
            disagree in places. The largest single correction raised a pixel by
            142.5 m, which is a 2018 tree polygon standing over a tower built
            since.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Fifteen frames are not fifteen equal hours.</strong> The
            06:00 frame sits at 0.38&deg; above the horizon and counts the
            whole city as shaded. Read every count in this guide as frames of
            the modelled day, not as hours of usable shade.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>One day of one year.</strong> Everything here is 21 July
            2026, 06:00 to 20:00 EDT. Chapter six adds one measurement at
            12:00 EST on 21 January 2026. It compares midday coverage and says
            nothing about a full winter day or season.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>The flight has a date.</strong> The lidar was flown
            April to May 2023, so anything built or felled since is missing.
            Roofs are inferred rather than measured, which matters most for
            shade cast from the tops of buildings.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>The grid is 2 m.</strong> A single narrow tree, a
            bus shelter, or a gap between two towers can fall between samples.
            The original plan called for 1 m and this run did not reach it.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>The walk band was chosen after both bands were
            computed</strong>, and that choice decides the title. Chapter four
            publishes the road band beside it so the choice can be checked
            rather than taken on trust.
          </li>
